significantly contribute to the downstream model's ability to distinguish between vehicle types. Dropping it reduces model complexity and prevents overfitting.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# HOLLOWS_RATIO has narrow variation (Std = 7.3390) with very close Q1, Median, and Q3 values, indicating it's relatively constant across samples. \n# Such features are less informative for prediction and can introduce noise into the model.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO is dropped\n# MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a highly limited range ([3, 52]), with a significant clustering near lower values (mean = 8.7199). \n# This diminishes its discriminative power in identifying different vehicle types.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n```end", "```python\n# Feature name: COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO\n# Feature description: The ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY.\n# Usefulness: This feature combines the measures of how compact and circular the silhouette is, potentially capturing differences between vehicle types. For example, circularity and compactness might be distinct for different shapes such as buses and vans versus cars with a more rounded profile.\ndf['COMPACTNESS_CIRCULARITY_RATIO'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] / df['CIRCULARITY']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: LOG_SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION\n# Feature description: The natural logarithm of SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION.\n# Usefulness: Log transformation helps reduce skewness and spread of features with a wide range of values (as in SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION). Capturing variations in the scaled radius of gyration may better distinguish between vehicles with different distribution densities, e.g., elongated versus compact vehicles.\ndf['LOG_SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION'] = df['SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION'].apply(np.log)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: MAJOR_MINOR_VARIANCE_DIFF\n# Feature description: The absolute difference between SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: By calculating the difference, this feature emphasizes the disparity in variances along the major and minor axes. Different vehicle silhouettes, such as buses versus sedans, may exhibit significantly different variance distributions due to the elongations in specific directions.\ndf['MAJOR_MINOR_VARIANCE_DIFF'] = abs(df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] - df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION is dropped\n# The feature LOG_SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION adds the same informative property of this column but is better scaled and less skewed, simplifying the model's interpretability and possibly improving performance.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# The feature MAJOR_MINOR_VARIANCE_DIFF incorporates the insights from the original column but in combination with SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS, rendering each of the variance columns redundant on their own.\ndf.drop(columns=['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
